					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lumancer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(662776); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=662776&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930918" alt="Lumancer" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=662776)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lumancer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(662776); return false;" >Lumancer</a></span> <span class="odate time_1398903643 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 May 2014 00:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2023159">
									

<p>I'd like to think that a key failure point on Game Day was that there were too many things being done by too many different authors in too many different places- after the initial tale things spread out to the point that they were impossible to follow and any semblance of overarching narrative gradually broke down. Author participation followed in suit.</p>
<p>If I understand the idea for Cavalcade correctly, that failure point will be avoided by keeping the creation process linear- a simple baton pass from author to author- without necessarily constraining the narrative in kind. If nothing else it seems like a worthwhile experiment, and the premise has some promise. I'm very much &quot;wait and see&quot; for now.</p>

							</div>

								
					
