														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2023246">
									

<p>The Cavalcade, I think, will have a few advantages over Game Day:</p>
<ul>
<li>There is no pre-defined plot that people can go off-topic from or deviate from, other than that which emerges by consensus.
<ul>
<li>Even then, no particular hook or subplot be followed up on if no writer finds it interesting.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Characters and plots are explicitly <em>not</em> the domain of any particular writer, allowing someone else to easily pick up the slack if love needs to be shown.</li>
<li>Plot lines will inevitably multiply such that people can follow up on whichever they want, or start their own. This freedom to choose will, hopefully, lead to better writing.</li>
<li>People aren't writing over each other onto the same page &#8212; as Lumancer says below, one author at a time, one order to read stories in.</li>
</ul>
<p>How redundant was all of that?</p>

