<p>To be brief, I will say that my above post is my response to an argument I had before.</p>
<p>If you take an SCP and transform it into something new or develop it past its original, then that SCP has broken the canon from its page.</p>
<p>The logical solution to fix the canon would be to edit that SCP page with the new information. However, SCP pages are considered &quot;final works&quot; and that what is there is final. Never before has a SCP been changed beyond the final work of that SCP on that SCP page (besides the old 808, but I do not want to get into that discussion). Thus, any tale that breaks away from the SCP page is changing canon.</p>
<p>Sure, tales have no canon, but SCP pages do have a canon in their own universe. In this tale, 315 is suggested to cause a XK-Class Scenario, when on the SCP page (the canon for 315) suggests nothing of the sort.</p>
<p>A tale of this nature thus does not work for me as canon is lost just because the author can do it. When writing a tale about an SCP, you have to stick in the confines of that SCP (this tale does not). The community should support this idea as I am doing by down voting. Otherwise what is the point of having any SCPs at all if anyone can just change them on a whim within a tale.</p>
<p>*Disclaimer: This is not my opinion, but merely what I was told on the IRC chat.</p>

							</div>

								
					
