				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1777811&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930918" alt="weizhong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1777811)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;" >weizhong</a></span> <span class="odate time_1398939117 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 May 2014 10:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2023390">
									

<p>I strongly disagree. The whole point of tales is that they flesh out the SCP universe in order to do things that SCPs can't express. If a tale about an SCP was exactly the same as the SCP, there would be no point in even reading it, because you know exactly what's gonna happen. A tale that doesn't build anything with an SCP to make it more interesting is utterly pointless, and completely boring.</p>
<p>There are acceptable deviations from SCPs. If you say that 682 is an extradimensional being that experienced genocide at the hands of some carbon-based life, sure, whatever. Directly violating the core of the piece and saying that 682 is a stuffed toy that kills people with laser farts is not okay, because that's entirely changing the original SCP, and doesn't build off of it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
