				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lunardiviner" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735002); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1735002&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930918" alt="Lunardiviner" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1735002)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lunardiviner" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735002); return false;" >Lunardiviner</a></span> <span class="odate time_1398940903 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 May 2014 10:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2023399">
									

<p>The whole point of tales is to flesh SCPs out within reason. It does not mean add outrageous things that may or may not be there. If those things were there, there would be details on those things on their page.</p>
<p>However, what I am saying is that you can add those things if Foundation has absolutely no way of knowing those things existed. There is no official head canon (or it is different for all of us). Remember, the site looks through the Foundations eyes only, and the Foundation is NOT all knowing. If a SCP started displaying new properties, in canon, that SCP's page would have to be updated.</p>
<p>I was not suggesting changing pages that are finished and posted on the main list. They would still be there for the world to see. I was merely suggesting creating new pages that updated those changes if the author of the tale that made those changes wanted too. I felt it would add an interesting depth to a story and would show how an SCP &quot;evolved&quot; over time. However, the idea was shot down quite venomously by our good people over on the IRC.</p>
<p>But I feel I have digressed way beyond the point of this topic. So I think we should leave it there.</p>

							</div>

								
