		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2023422)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2023422">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1324229"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1398945312 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 May 2014 11:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2023422">
									

<blockquote>
<p>If you take an SCP and transform it into something new or develop it past its original, then that SCP has broken the canon from its page.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>1) What in God's holy name are you talking about? SCPs aren't these singular, discrete things bound to a single interpretation. Look how many different interpretations of 173 there are. Or, hell, even more recent stuff like Lord Blackwood.<br />
2) Even assuming you're correct (which you aren't), who cares? Does this somehow lessen your experience of reading the SCP in question?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The logical solution to fix the canon would be to edit that SCP page with the new information.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What? Why?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP pages are considered &quot;final works&quot; and that what is there is final.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By whom? I've been a member for over two years, and this is the first time I have ever heard anyone say this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thus, any tale that breaks away from the SCP page is changing canon.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Say it with me now: there is no canon. You can read multiple, differing interpretations of numerous SCPs on the site, and no one version is more correct than any other version. That's the entire point of not having a central, fixed canon<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-16571-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-16571-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<blockquote>
<p>When writing a tale about an SCP, you have to stick in the confines of that SCP</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is patently false, and I defy you to point to anyone besides yourself who's been here more than a month who <em>actually</em> claims this. If I want to make a story about 106 being a withered sailor scout who fights crime and resides in a jellybean castle, and the rest of the community likes it, then that's pretty much the end of it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Otherwise what is the point of having any SCPs at all if anyone can just change them on a whim within a tale.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I dunno, creative inspiration? Having something interesting and thought-provoking to read? But right, <em>I forgot we have a fixed canon, dictated by the contents of all of the SCP articles on the site.</em> Which often posit wildly varying interpretations of the Foundation, various groups of interest, and the nature of the Foundationverse itself.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-16571-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-16571-1')">1</a>. Beyond basic stuff like &quot;the Foundation exists and contains anomalous stuff.&quot; And <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/unfounded-hub|">even then...</a></div>
</div>

							</div>

								
